From 21e2b965057110bb308e0aadc1655662c87698e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: webby <webby@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 21:44:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] Windows port: show video decoded on the GPU without
 bringing it into memory

A frame decoded by Direct3D 11 used to be read back into system memory, copied
into shared memory for the GPU process and uploaded as a texture again: three
copies of every frame, which a 1440p 60fps stream could not afford, and most of
a CPU core at 1080p.

The frame now stays a Direct3D texture all the way to WebKit's video sink. On
the streaming thread the player copies it, on the GPU, into one of a few
textures created to be shared, waits for that copy, and hands the layer the
texture's share handle. The compositor in the GPU process opens the handle
through ANGLE (EGL_ANGLE_d3d_share_handle_client_buffer), binds it to a GL
texture once and draws it.

Painting a video the other way (into a canvas, or when it has no layer) reads
the frame back by mapping it. An image made from a sample keeps the sample
mapped, and a mapped Direct3D frame holds the lock of the decoder's device, so
that path copies the pixels out and unmaps at once.

WEBKIT_GST_D3D11_DOWNLOAD_FRAMES=1 goes back to reading frames into memory.

gst/d3d11/gstd3d11config.h is a header GStreamer's build generates and the
vcpkg port does not install; a copy with the values of a desktop build is added
where the include path finds it.
